ould narrow the margin.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Michelle Lujan Grisham (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "New Mexico's governorship is open as two-term Democrat Michelle Lujan Grisham is term-limited. Former Interior Secretary Deb Haaland, running in a state that leans Democratic by four points, faces Rio Rancho mayor Gregg Hull, a little-known Republican. Haaland has raised about $11.1 million to Hull's $474,000, giving her a dominant advertising advantage, and no significant outside spending has been reported on either side.\n\nThree polls conducted between mid-July and late August showed Democratic leads of one, six, and seventeen points, averaging near eight; independent aggregates put the race closer to ten or eleven. Seven raters call the race Solid or Likely Democratic, and prediction markets price a Democratic win at roughly 94 to 96 percent. Haaland's decision to skip debates is the main tactical risk, but the structural picture strongly favors her.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9664, "margin": 10.58}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9571, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9605, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.948, "margin": 10.2}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9415, "margin": 9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9502, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9552, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9463, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9506, "margin": 9.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.9559, "margin": 9.5}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.9507, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.9606, "margin": 10.0}]}